A network inventory system would typically model a FlexE interface carrying multiple low-rate clients over a high-rate physical port by creating a hierarchical structure, where the high-rate physical port is represented as a parent interface, and the low-rate clients are represented as child interfaces, each with a specific FlexE subinterface (FlexE-Subinterface) configured with a unique VLAN ID and a specific bandwidth allocation. This can be achieved using a data model like YANG, where the FlexE interface is represented as a container with a list of child interfaces, each with a VLAN ID and bandwidth allocation, as shown in the following example: 

```
container flexe-interfaces {
  list flexe-subinterface {
    key "vlan-id";
    leaf vlan-id {
      type uint32;
    }
    leaf bandwidth {
      type uint32;
    }
  }
}
```

Example configuration:
```
interface FlexE 0/0/0 {
  flexe-subinterface 1 {
    vlan-id 100;
    bandwidth 100000;
  }
  flexe-subinterface 2 {
    vlan-id 200;
    bandwidth 200000;
  }
}
```

This hierarchical structure allows for efficient management and monitoring of the FlexE interface, including bandwidth allocation and VLAN configuration for each client.

