Using an API Gateway for managing inter-service communication in a microservices architecture provides a centralized entry point for incoming requests, allowing for features such as request routing, rate limiting, and authentication, whereas a Service Mesh, like Istio or Linkerd, provides a more fine-grained control over service-to-service communication, including traffic management, security, and observability, but typically requires more infrastructure overhead and complexity. 

To implement an API Gateway, you can use a tool like NGINX, with a configuration like: 
```
http {
    ...
    upstream microservices {
        server localhost:8081;
        server localhost:8082;
    }
    server {
        listen 80;
        location / {
            proxy_pass http://microservices;
            proxy_set_header Host $host;
            proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        }
    }
}
```
This configuration sets up an NGINX server to route incoming requests to a pool of microservices running on ports 8081 and 8082.

