Feature Toggles can be used as part of a Strangler pattern by introducing a new microservice that initially serves a small subset of users or functionality, and then gradually increases its scope by toggling on/off specific features or user groups using a centralized feature flag management system, such as a feature toggle service like LaunchDarkly or feature flagging libraries like Spring Cloud Config or ASP.NET Core Feature Management, which can be integrated with the application code using a configuration file or API calls. For example, in a Spring Boot application, you can use the @FeatureToggle annotation to conditionally enable or disable features based on the feature flag status, as shown in the following code snippet: 
```java
@FeatureToggle("new-feature")
public class NewFeatureService {
    // feature implementation
}
```
This allows you to control the rollout of the new feature to a subset of users or environments, and gradually phase out the legacy system as the new microservice becomes more robust and widely adopted.

