NETCONF and gNMI (gRPC Network Management Interface) are both modern southbound interfaces used for network management, but they differ in their architecture and approach. NETCONF is a protocol-based interface that uses XML or JSON encoding to exchange configuration and operational data, whereas gNMI is a protocol-agnostic interface that uses gRPC as the transport and supports multiple encoding formats, including JSON and Protocol Buffers. 

NETCONF configuration example:
```
<rpc message-id="101">
  <edit-config>
    <target>
      <candidate/>
    </target>
    <config>
      <interfaces>
        <interface>
          <name>eth0</name>
          <description>Management interface</description>
        </interface>
      </interfaces>
    </config>
  </edit-config>
</rpc>
```
gNMI configuration example (using gRPC and JSON encoding):
```
// Define the gNMI service
service NMDA {
  rpc get  {
    rpc Get  (GetRequest) returns (GetResponse) {}
  }
}

// Define the GetRequest message
message GetRequest {
  string path = 1;
  string filter = 2;
}

// Define the GetResponse message
message GetResponse {
  string path = 1;
  string value = 2;
}

// Create a gNMI client
client = gNMI::Client.new("localhost:50051")

// Send a GetRequest
request = GetRequest.new(path: "/interfaces/interface[name='eth0']", filter: "")
response = client.get(request)

// Print the response
puts response.value
```
In terms of scalability and performance, gNMI is generally considered more efficient and scalable than NETCONF due to its use of gRPC and Protocol Buffers, which provide better performance and lower latency. However, NETCONF has a larger ecosystem and more established tooling, making it a more widely adopted standard.

