A VNFM (Virtual Network Function Manager) or EM (Orchestration Manager) can use NETCONF and YANG to configure a VNF instance by sending a NETCONF request to the VNF's NETCONF server, which is typically implemented using a NETCONF agent, such as the OpenDaylight controller or a VNF-specific NETCONF agent. The request is encoded in XML or JSON format, using a YANG model that defines the VNF's configuration data, and is sent to the NETCONF server using the NETCONF protocol, which then applies the configuration changes to the VNF instance.

Example YANG model for VNF configuration:
```yml
module: example-vnf-config
namespace: urn:example:vnf:config
prefix: vnf-cfg

container vnf {
  description "VNF configuration";
  container vnf-instance {
    description "VNF instance configuration";
    leaf vnf-name {
      type string;
      description "Name of the VNF instance";
    }
    leaf vnf-version {
      type string;
      description "Version of the VNF instance";
    }
  }
}
```
Example NETCONF request to configure a VNF instance:
```xml
<config xmlns="urn:ietf:params:xml:ns:netconf:base:1.0">
  <example-vnf-config:vnf xmlns:example-vnf-config="urn:example:vnf:config">
    <example-vnf-config:vnf-instance>
      <example-vnf-config:vnf-name>example-vnf</example-vnf-config:vnf-name>
      <example-vnf-config:vnf-version>1.0</example-vnf-config:vnf-version>
    </example-vnf-config:vnf-instance>
  </example-vnf-config:vnf>
</config>
```
This example demonstrates how a VNFM or EM can use NETCONF and YANG to configure a VNF instance by sending a NETCONF request to the VNF's NETCONF server, which applies the configuration changes to the VNF instance.

